      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Woodbury‑to‑Glassboro rail line opened in 1861, putting a station half a mile from Malaga’s main street and linking it to Camden, Millville, and Cape May.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malaga_new_jersey",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the mid‑1800s, Malaga supported about 60 settler families and boasted a glass factory that employed 100‑200 workers, making it a regional industrial hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malaga_new_jersey",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Malaga’s landscape features flat uplands with sandy, well‑drained soils like Aura sandy loam, supporting local farms and aiding groundwater recharge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malaga_new_jersey",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Old Malaga Road, surveyed early 19th century, connected Millville to Malaga and helped move lumber, charcoal, and produce to Philadelphia after the War of 1812.",
